 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "in-plain-english",
   "heading": "In plain English",
   "role": "intuition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "in-plain-english-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Integration by parts is the product rule run backwards: it trades one integral that is hard to compute for another that is easier. In economics its most useful job is computing averages from distributions — for example, showing that the average of a positive quantity equals the area under the curve of the probability that it exceeds each value. The same area logic turns a Lorenz curve into a Gini coefficient."
      },
      {
       "t": "callout",
       "kind": "why",
       "title": "Why it matters",
       "md": "It is the standard tool for expectations, moments and areas under distribution curves.",
       "folded": false
      }
     ],
     "words": 86,
     "symbols": []
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "definition",
   "heading": "Definition",
   "role": "definition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "definition-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "The product rule, integrated:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "\\int_a^b u\\,dv = \\big[uv\\big]_a^b - \\int_a^b v\\,du"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Choose $u$ so that $du$ is simpler than $u$, and $dv$ so that $v$ is easy to find."
      }
     ],
     "words": 17,
     "symbols": [
      "$u$, $v$",
      "$a$, $b$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "worked-example-expectation-from-the-survival-function",
   "heading": "Worked example — expectation from the survival function",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "worked-example-expectation-from-the-survival-function-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "For $X \\geq 0$ with CDF $F$ and density $f$:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "E[X] = \\int_0^\\infty x f(x)\\,dx = \\int_0^\\infty \\big(1 - F(x)\\big)\\,dx"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Take $u = x$ and $dv = f(x)\\,dx$ with $v = -(1 - F(x))$. The boundary term $-x(1-F(x))$ vanishes at both ends for distributions with a finite mean."
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "For an exponential distribution with rate 0.5, $1 - F(x) = e^{-0.5x}$, and the integral gives $E[X] = 2$."
      }
     ],
     "words": 33,
     "symbols": [
      "$u$, $v$",
      "$X$",
      "$F$, $f$",
      "$E[X]$",
      "$x$, $u$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "areas-in-inequality",
   "heading": "Areas in inequality",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "areas-in-inequality-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "With a continuous [Lorenz Curve](note:lorenz-curve) $L(p)$, the area between the line of equality and $L$ gives the [Gini Coefficient](note:gini-coefficient):"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "G = 1 - 2\\int_0^1 L(p)\\,dp"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "If $L(p) = p^2$, then $\\int_0^1 p^2\\,dp = 1/3$ and $G = 1/3$. Integrating by parts, with $L'(p)$ equal to income at rank $p$ relative to the mean, gives the equivalent covariance form $G = 2\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(y, F(y))/\\mu$ (a standard result, beyond these notes)."
      }
     ],
     "words": 44,
     "symbols": [
      "$F$, $f$",
      "$L(p)$",
      "$G$",
      "$y$, $\\mu$",
      "$p$, $c$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "common-mistakes",
   "heading": "Common mistakes",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "common-mistakes-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- Choosing $u$ and $dv$ the hard way round (e.g. $u = e^x$, $dv = x\\,dx$ makes it worse).\n- Dropping the boundary term without checking that it vanishes."
      }
     ],
     "words": 22,
     "symbols": [
      "$u$, $v$",
      "$x$, $u$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "where-it-is-used",
   "heading": "Where it is used",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "where-it-is-used-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- **Probability / econometrics**: moments of distributions; deriving the mean of truncated distributions.\n- **Inequality**: Gini and Lorenz-curve integrals.\n- **Labor / search**: expected wage above a reservation wage, $\\int_{w_R}^\\infty (1 - F(w))\\,dw$."
      }
     ],
